  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Hotelier Adrian Zecha first built a business in magazine publishing. After working for Time, he raised international capital to launch Asia Magazine as a newspaper supplement. To recruit experienced journalists, he reserved a quarter of the company for employee stock options. Printing in Tokyo meant copies had to be shipped six weeks ahead of publication.\n\nHis interest in Asian ceramics developed into a substantial collection assembled over two decades. It brought together Chinese export wares and pottery from Vietnam and Thailand, including blue-and-white jars and celadon dishes. A founding member of the Southeast Asian Ceramics Society, he later offered the collection through a dedicated auction.",
    "html": "<p>Hotelier Adrian Zecha first built a business in magazine publishing. After working for Time, he raised international capital to launch Asia Magazine as a newspaper supplement. To recruit experienced journalists, he reserved a quarter of the company for employee stock options. Printing in Tokyo meant copies had to be shipped six weeks ahead of publication.</p>\r\n<p>His interest in Asian ceramics developed into a substantial collection assembled over two decades. It brought together Chinese export wares and pottery from Vietnam and Thailand, including blue-and-white jars and celadon dishes. A founding member of the Southeast Asian Ceramics Society, he later offered the collection through a dedicated auction.</p>\r\n",
